-- title:
--   Comaximal residual ideals split the quotient and admit complementary idempotents
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative $\mathbb C$-algebra, and let $I\subseteq J$ be ideals of $A$. Define the residual by the entire ideal $J$:
--
--   $$R=I:J=\{a\in A:aJ\subseteq I\}.$$
--
--   Suppose $J+R=A$. Then
--
--   $$J\cap R=I,\qquad JR=I,$$
--
--   and there is a $\mathbb C$-algebra isomorphism
--
--   $$A/I\;\cong\;(A/J)\times(A/R).$$
--
--   Moreover, there is $p\in A$ with
--
--   $$p\in J,\qquad 1-p\in R,\qquad p^2-p\in I,$$
--
--   such that
--
--   $$J=I+(p),\qquad R=I+(1-p).$$
--
--   Thus the quotient splits as a product, and the two complementary ideals in $A/I$ are generated by complementary idempotents.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The only assumptions are that $A$ is a commutative complex algebra, $I\subseteq J$, and $J+R=A$ for the stated colon ideal. No finiteness, reducedness, principality, contact, time-polynomial or lattice hypothesis is added. The idempotence is modulo $I$: $p^2-p\in I$, not necessarily $p^2=p$ in $A$. The two generation equalities are also modulo $I$. The theorem includes $I=J$, $J=A$ and the zero algebra. It asserts existence of an algebra isomorphism, not an additional chosen map or generator.
-- source:
--   Derived commutative-algebra step for the contact-ideal approach associated with Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A introductory paragraphs and Theorem A.2, https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. This exact residual-splitting statement is proved here, not quoted from the article. Colon membership gives J*(I:J) contained in I; comaximality makes product equal intersection and hence equal I. The binary Chinese remainder theorem gives the algebra product. Writing 1=p+q with p in J and q in the residual yields p^2-p in I and the two generators modulo I. Primary formal references: Ideal.mul_eq_inf_of_isCoprime, Ideal.quotientInfEquivQuotientProd and its two projection lemmas, Ideal.quotientEquivAlgOfEq, Submodule.mem_colon and Submodule.mem_sup.

import Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic
import Mathlib.Algebra.Algebra.Prod
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Colon
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Quotient.Operations

open scoped Classical

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.comaximal_residual_decomposition
    (A : Type*) [CommRing A] [Algebra ℂ A]
    (I J : Ideal A) (hIJ : I ≤ J) :
    let R := I.colon (J : Set A)
    J ⊔ R = ⊤ →
      J ⊓ R = I ∧ J * R = I ∧
      Nonempty ((A ⧸ I) ≃ₐ[ℂ] (A ⧸ J) × (A ⧸ R)) ∧
      ∃ p : A,
        p ∈ J ∧ 1 - p ∈ R ∧ p * p - p ∈ I ∧
        J = I ⊔ Ideal.span {p} ∧ R = I ⊔ Ideal.span {1 - p} := by sorry
